  "novel_mechanics_found": "Level 7 has two simultaneously required perpendicular pairs but no fixed blocker. The horizontal target is 9,14,9 and the vertical target is 8,14,8. Only five free rack blocks exist for the six target positions, so the sole free color-14 block must be shared at the chains' intersection. The successful construction moves the intersection to (41,32), where both chains see that same 14 in their middle slot; the two 9 blocks flank it horizontally and the two 8 blocks flank it vertically.",
  "narrative": "The mandated resolver selected results/outer_loop_round11_sk48_probe_20260712.json. A fresh env.reset() followed by all 268 resolved labels reproduced frame_level 6 with GameState.NOT_FINISHED exactly. Compact component and internal settled-state dumps mapped level 7 as two checked pairs: horizontal 9,14,9 and vertical 8,14,8, with five free rack blocks and no fixed blocker. Because the targets require six positions but the free rack supplies only one 14, I inferred and then searched for a shared crossing block. An exact source-grounded abstract transition search found a 24-action tail, which was checked against deep-copied actual game logic before any additional live calls. The solution first shifts the vertical chain and uses its extension/retraction asymmetry to stage both 8 blocks and relocate the 9/14 material, then completes the horizontal 9,14,9 order at y=32 across x=35,41,47. Finally the vertical chain at x=41 extends through 8,14,8, sharing the 14 at (41,32). A newly constructed offline environment replayed the complete 292-label sequence from reset and independently reproduced transitions 0->1 at label 14, 1->2 at 44, 2->3 at 81, 3->4 at 106, 4->5 at 144, 5->6 at 268, and 6->7 at 292, ending GameState.NOT_FINISHED.",
  "gotchas": [
    "The visually obvious crossing at the original 14 position (35,32) is an ordering-contamination trap: the nearer 8 block can enter the horizontal chain before the intended 9,14,9 sequence is established.",
    "Both target pairs need three entries even though there are only five free rack blocks; treating the targets as disjoint makes the level appear impossible. The middle 14 must be seen by both chains simultaneously.",
    "The successful crossing is deliberately relocated to (41,32). At the end, the horizontal chain sees 9 at (35,32), shared 14 at (41,32), and 9 at (47,32), while the vertical chain sees 8 at (41,26), the same 14, and 8 at (41,44).",
    "ACTION6 selection is geometry-neutral and does not spend energy, but its click coordinates must identify the correct static paired head: (35,56) selects the vertical pair and (5,56) selects the horizontal pair.",
    "Boundary jamming remains load-bearing: the final lower 8 is pushed to y=44 and stays fixed while the vertical endpoint extends into it, producing the third ordered observation."
  ],
